text:
Yes. It's not just possible — it's expected behavior given the current code. Any logged-in user can edit anyone's article.

## The code path

`PUT /articles/:slug` **is** behind auth, so it wasn't an anonymous request:

`src/article/article.module.ts:27` — the route is in the `AuthMiddleware` list, so a request with no token gets a 401.

But the middleware only answers *"is this a valid user?"*, never *"is this **the** user?"* — and the handler never even asks who's calling:

```ts
// src/article/article.controller.ts:59
@Put(':slug')
async update(@Param() params, @Body('article') articleData: CreateArticleDto) {
  return this.articleService.update(params.slug, articleData);
}
```

Note there's no `@User('id')` parameter at all — unlike `create`, `favorite`, and `unFavorite` right below it, which all take one. The caller's identity is discarded before the service is reached, so the service couldn't check ownership even if it wanted to:

```ts
// src/article/article.service.ts:190
async update(slug: string, articleData: any): Promise<ArticleRO> {
  let toUpdate = await this.articleRepository.findOne({ slug: slug});
  let updated = Object.assign(toUpdate, articleData);
  const article = await this.articleRepository.save(updated);
  return {article};
}
```

Look up slug → overwrite → save. `ArticleEntity` has an `author` relation (`article.entity.ts:37`), and it is never compared against anything.

So: **any valid token + your slug = edit.** Your slug is public, from `GET /articles`.

## It's worse than just the title and body

There's no `ValidationPipe` on `ArticleController` — the app has one (`src/shared/pipes/validation.pipe.ts`) but it's applied per handler, and only `UserController` uses it (lines 31, 42). Nothing is registered globally in `main.ts`. Combined with `Object.assign` and the service signature being `articleData: any`, every key in the request body's `article` object lands on the entity. That includes `id`, `author`, `slug`, `created`, and `favoriteCount` — so a caller could reassign authorship of your article to themselves, or change the slug to break inbound links.

The `@ApiResponse({ status: 403 })` decorators on these handlers describe intent, not implementation. Nothing in the article module returns 403.

Two adjacent holes with the same root cause, worth knowing about while you're triaging:

- `DELETE /articles/:slug` (`controller.ts:68`) — same missing check. Whoever edited it could have deleted it.
- `DELETE /users/:slug` — not in any middleware list, so it needs **no token at all**, and deletes the user whose email matches the param.

## On finding out who did it

The database can't tell you. `@BeforeUpdate()` (`article.entity.ts:29`) stamps `updated`, so you have a *when*, but nothing records *who* performed an update — there's no audit table and the author column is only written at creation. If you have a reverse proxy or hosting platform retaining HTTP access logs, correlating the request to `PUT /api/articles/<your-slug>` against that `updated` timestamp is the only trail that exists. The JWTs are signed with `SECRET` from `src/config.ts` and last 60 days, which also matters here: if that secret has ever been committed or shared, forged tokens are on the table too, and the attacker wouldn't need a real account.

I'd treat this as an active vulnerability rather than a one-off incident.

Want me to fix it? The minimal change is to thread `@User('id')` into `update` and `delete`, load the article with `relations: ['author']`, and throw a 403 on mismatch — plus whitelisting the update to `title`/`description`/`body`/`tagList` instead of the bare `Object.assign`. I'd do the article routes and the unauthenticated `DELETE /users/:slug` in one pass, since they're the same class of bug.
